<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ensisheim</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ensisheim is a small town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, north of Mulhouse. From the late 15th century until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> government of Further <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, with a regency and treasury for the western lands. It is known for the Ensisheim meteorite, which fell in 1492 and is the oldest recorded meteorite fall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> whose stone survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ensisheim.jpg" alt="Ensisheim" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rauenstein · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ensisheim,_Mairie_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ensisheim is a commune in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haut-rhin">Haut-Rhin</a> department in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. People lived in the area long before the town existed. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> burial site near it yielded the skeleton of a man of about 50 whose skull showed two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trepanning">trepanation</a> operations, one fully healed and the other partly, so that he had survived both. Researchers from Freiburg University reported in 1997 that the remains date to between 5100 and 4900 BCE, which makes it one of the earliest clear examples of the practice. The village is first mentioned in 768, as Enghisehaim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the second half of the 13th century Rudolph I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> built a castle at Ensisheim that came to be known as the Königsburg. Rabbi Meir of Rothenburg was held captive there for seven years until his death in 1293. On 28 October 1444 the Pact of Ensisheim, connected with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-st-jakob-an-der-birs">Battle of St. Jakob an der Birs</a>, was signed in the town by the Confederation, Zurich and the French mercenaries.</p><p>On 7 November 1492 a meteorite fell into a field close to the town. Sebastian Brant, author of The Ship of Fools, used the occasion to write Loose Leaves Concerning the Fall of the Meteorite, in which he sought to influence Maximilian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and the king went on to pursue a war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The administrative and legal functions of the town brought it a prosperity that peaked between the end of the 16th and the early 17th century. It had a mint from 1585 to 1634, the most significant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> after Strasbourg.</p><p>The war that began in 1618 brought decline. The imperial administration left Ensisheim in 1630, and the town was pillaged seven times between 1631 and 1638. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> possessions passed to the French Crown, which set up a Royal Chamber and then, in 1662, a Provincial Council. When imperial troops returned in 1674 the council withdrew to Breisach, and Marshal Turenne retook the town the following year. Ensisheim was afterwards only the chief town of a bailliage until the Revolution. At the start of the 20th century the extraction of potash brought it a new rise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The meteorite, which fell in 1492, can still be seen in the town's museum, the Musée de la Régence. The composer Léon Boëllmann was born in Ensisheim. The town is twinned with Markdorf in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, Castroville in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and Otjiwarongo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, and it belongs to the Communauté de communes du Centre Haut-Rhin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ensisheim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A stony meteorite fell in a wheat field outside the walled town of <b>Ensisheim</b> in Alsace.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-7">7 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It was governed from <b>Ensisheim</b> and later Freiburg, often together with the County of Tyrol, and had its own estates.</blockquote><a href="/state/further-austria">Further Austria</a></li></ul></section>
